 ## Preserved numerical limitations
 
 The historical g=1.8 affine challenge retained an approximately1.8e-7 derivative discrepancy at its smaller dual cutoff; it was not a rigorous tail interval. Original numerical records, including cutoff limitations, remain byte-exact in recovery. The charged-ring calculation measures off-space leakage separately from compression error; a small compressed error does not bound that leakage.
+
+## Appendix: volume and infrared scale are separate questions
+
+This diagnostic is an exact identity inside the supplied charged rotor model, not a separate scientific milestone or axiom no-go. At fixed g>0, write P_e=g E_e. Exact Gauss law implies, for every finite vertex test f and every real t,
+
+    exp[i t sum_e (D* f)_e P_e] = exp[i t g sum_x f_x Q_x]
+
+on the physical space. Both observables are bounded and local if f has finite support. In a free transverse Maxwell gauge state the left expectation is one. A free matter state with a nontrivial distribution of Q(f) has a right characteristic function different from one for some t. Thus merely increasing volume at fixed g cannot yield the decoupled local Maxwell/free-matter state specified by those two reference properties, with those same observables and fixed normalization. This conclusion does not require convergence of unbounded electric moments.
+
+An infrared rescaling changes the probes and may also induce a running effective coupling. The identity above does not rule out an emergent free or weakly interacting macroscopic limit. Proving that limit requires the appropriately scaled charge/current correlations and their coupling to the gauge field. The Coulomb term in the new variational compression preserves this distinction.
+
+This argument belongs to the supplied-model Gauss-dressing result above. The operator identity follows by exponentiating the exact Gauss constraint on the physical space; the mismatch concerns fixed local bounded probes at fixed g and excludes no rescaled macroscopic theory.
